David Mendenhall: What's the symbolism of the bottom right?

673
00:48:35.510 --> 00:48:38.710
Kostadis Roussos: Yeah, you got me worried there, man.

674
00:48:38.950 --> 00:48:39.349
Kostadis Roussos: All right.

675
00:48:40.230 --> 00:48:41.030
Kostadis Roussos: So, okay.

676
00:48:41.990 --> 00:48:49.269
Kostadis Roussos: So just to remind you, so Neverwinter managed to survive the storm known as the War of the Dragons.

677
00:48:49.430 --> 00:48:55.269
Kostadis Roussos: It managed to survive the storm known as the War of the Giants.

678
00:48:55.590 --> 00:49:02.309
Kostadis Roussos: And now, after several managed to survive the barbarian hordes that made it.

679
00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:03.990
David Mendenhall: Is that what's been going on?

680
00:49:04.309 --> 00:49:06.470
David Mendenhall: For like the last five years, that's been happening?

681
00:49:07.190 --> 00:49:07.990
David Mendenhall: I missed all of that.

682
00:49:08.550 --> 00:49:08.950
Wade Brown [?]: Yes, yes.

683
00:49:09.269 --> 00:49:11.030
Kostadis Roussos [?]: The land has been crying out.

684
00:49:11.349 --> 00:49:11.590
UNKNOWN: Right.

685
00:49:12.390 --> 00:49:31.510
Kostadis Roussos: Neverwinter had, you know, Neverwinter was strategically placed sufficiently far away from the hordes of the Uthgardtian barbarians that after the War of the Giants ended, was able to avoid the worst of the tribal wars.

686
00:49:32.150 --> 00:49:34.870
Gary Young: Yeah, the barbarians.

687
00:49:34.950 --> 00:49:35.430
Gary Young: Yeah, that was.

688
00:49:38.950 --> 00:49:41.829
Kostadis Roussos: But now, now, now, what is it?

689
00:49:42.710 --> 00:49:49.590
Kostadis Roussos: Now, Neverwinter is all about growth, advancing, making things better.

690
00:49:49.910 --> 00:49:58.630
Kostadis Roussos: And, you know, after having had some trouble to the north with its hinterland, it's proposing to re-establish trade and outposts to the south.

691
00:49:59.590 --> 00:50:01.510
Kostadis Roussos: And one of those is Phandalin.

692
00:50:02.230 --> 00:50:04.470
Kostadis Roussos: Phandalin has been having trouble.

693
00:50:04.789 --> 00:50:13.990
Kostadis Roussos: And because Lord Neverember, well, there's limited budget, limited limited member, limited resources.

694
00:50:14.309 --> 00:50:19.190
Kostadis Roussos: And, you know, he's come up with a brilliant scheme, hiring adventures.

695
00:50:20.150 --> 00:50:25.430
Kostadis Roussos: For those of you in the management biz, he's going with contractors because they have fewer benefits.

696
00:50:25.750 --> 00:50:26.710
Gary Young: That's our thing.

697
00:50:30.519 --> 00:50:38.359
Kostadis Roussos: And as his convention, anybody who does a good turn for the kingdom can keep whatever the gold and treasure they want, etc.

698
00:50:41.820 --> 00:50:45.099
Kostadis Roussos: Well, if you ever had enough money for taxes, that's what he meant.

699
00:50:45.260 --> 00:50:47.900
David Mendenhall: Oh, no, it's tax-free income, is what you're telling me.

700
00:50:48.219 --> 00:50:49.340
Wade Brown [?]: Yes, it's tax-free.

701
00:50:49.659 --> 00:50:51.980
Wade Brown: Well, that's not typical for $10.99.

702
00:50:52.940 --> 00:50:54.139
David Mendenhall: Okay, well.

703
00:50:55.900 --> 00:50:56.619
Kostadis Roussos: So the paper.

704
00:50:57.500 --> 00:51:12.380
Kostadis Roussos: Yeah, so the paper is calling all adventurers to head south, to go clear the hinterland of monsters, enemies of Neverwinter, and re-establish civilization and the march of progress.

705
00:51:12.699 --> 00:51:15.340
David Mendenhall: And after we clear a monster, then what do we do?

706
00:51:15.980 --> 00:51:17.500
Wade Brown: Do you bring their scalps?

707
00:51:17.820 --> 00:51:18.860
Wade Brown: Seems vile.

708
00:51:19.420 --> 00:51:21.659
David Mendenhall: Do we take them to Lord Neverember?

709
00:51:21.980 --> 00:51:22.940
Kostadis Roussos: No, no, no.

710
00:51:24.699 --> 00:51:26.699
Wade Brown: Like a cat delivering a dead mouse.

711
00:51:27.340 --> 00:51:28.780
Kostadis Roussos: Well, so I'm going to assume that you're.

712
00:51:29.099 --> 00:51:48.539
Kostadis Roussos: I'm going to assume that as you pick up this notice, a guard is standing next, a guard is standing next to you in Neverwinter and says, looks at you with this quirky expression and says, no, please, for the love of goodness, all that is good, do not bring anything to his lordship.

713
00:51:49.260 --> 00:51:49.820
Kostadis Roussos: Why do you ask?

714
00:51:49.900 --> 00:51:51.579
Kostadis Roussos: Are you asking about how you make the money?

715
00:51:51.900 --> 00:51:52.300
David Mendenhall: Yes.

716
00:51:54.460 --> 00:52:02.300
Kostadis Roussos: He pauses and goes, well, I am, of course, an official city guard and get paid on a regular basis, have benefits.

717
00:52:02.940 --> 00:52:03.820
David Mendenhall: So, like that.

718
00:52:04.139 --> 00:52:04.460
Kostadis Roussos: I'm off.

719
00:52:06.940 --> 00:52:10.139
Kostadis Roussos: That is not how adventures get paid.

720
00:52:10.460 --> 00:52:14.300
Kostadis Roussos: You are an adventurer, or at least this is what we're looking to hire.

721
00:52:14.619 --> 00:52:22.699
Kostadis Roussos: And the way they make money is by defeating the monsters and securing whatever trinkets and treasure they might have amongst their goods.

722
00:52:25.900 --> 00:52:26.940
Wade Brown [?]: So we just loot them.

723
00:52:27.579 --> 00:52:30.059
David Mendenhall: You just take the money from the monsters?

724
00:52:30.699 --> 00:52:31.179
David Mendenhall: Yes.

725
00:52:32.699 --> 00:52:34.380
Kostadis Roussos: It's an excellent system.

726
00:52:34.699 --> 00:52:35.739
Kostadis Roussos: We get what we want.

727
00:52:36.139 --> 00:52:38.139
David Mendenhall: Where did the monsters get that money from?

728
00:52:38.699 --> 00:52:39.420
Kostadis Roussos: I don't know.

729
00:52:39.579 --> 00:52:43.019
Kostadis Roussos: And to be quite honest with you, Lord Neverember and the guard don't care.

730
00:52:43.340 --> 00:52:44.619
David Mendenhall: Yeah, but I care.

731
00:52:47.500 --> 00:52:52.780
Kostadis Roussos: You aren't going to be very wealthy in this adventuring business if you care too much about these details.

732
00:52:53.659 --> 00:52:56.219
David Mendenhall: Well, now, I'm not super dumb.

733
00:52:56.380 --> 00:53:01.579
David Mendenhall: I mean, I can imagine the monsters got the money from killing people.

734
00:53:02.219 --> 00:53:02.780
David Mendenhall: Yes.

735
00:53:03.739 --> 00:53:09.019
David Mendenhall: And so, rightfully, that money belongs to their heirs.

736
00:53:10.619 --> 00:53:11.099
David Mendenhall[?]: Yes.

737
00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:14.539
David Mendenhall: So we'll be returning the money to their heirs.

738
00:53:14.860 --> 00:53:15.579
Kostadis Roussos: If you wish to remain.

739
00:53:15.820 --> 00:53:21.099
Kostadis Roussos: Yes, but the guard says, You, sir, are you sure you're not a paladin?

740
00:53:25.929 --> 00:53:26.730
Kostadis Roussos: A paladin.

741
00:53:27.529 --> 00:53:32.009
David Mendenhall: I'm just very confused about why we would be trafficking in stolen goods.

742
00:53:32.170 --> 00:53:33.289
David Mendenhall: Aren't you a guard?

743
00:53:36.719 --> 00:53:37.599
Kostadis Roussos: He pauses.

744
00:53:40.239 --> 00:53:41.439
Kostadis Roussos: You are absolutely correct.

745
00:53:41.679 --> 00:53:43.599
Kostadis Roussos: However, we view it in a different way.

746
00:53:44.960 --> 00:53:52.479
Kostadis Roussos: Outside of the civilized boundaries of Neverwinter's walls, the laws of the laws of Neverwinter do not apply.

747
00:53:52.639 --> 00:53:59.519
Kostadis Roussos: And that is why we need you to go and establish civilization to pacify the areas so that the laws can be done.

748
00:53:59.679 --> 00:54:03.039
David Mendenhall: And once the civilization is established, we should follow those laws.

749
00:54:03.359 --> 00:54:07.279
Kostadis Roussos: Yes, but until then, we are in the law of the hinterland.

750
00:54:07.439 --> 00:54:09.439
David Mendenhall: This seems like very grayish to me.

751
00:54:10.559 --> 00:54:11.839
Kostadis Roussos: He points to the city wall.

752
00:54:12.239 --> 00:54:14.559
David Mendenhall[?]: The gray begins at the wall.

753
00:54:19.439 --> 00:54:23.279
Kostadis Roussos [?]: I think we should return the money to the rightful owners.

754
00:54:25.119 --> 00:54:31.509
Kostadis Roussos: The guardsman's eyes sparkle and says, I have an even better idea.

755
00:54:31.829 --> 00:54:34.630
Kostadis Roussos: The city guard has a retirement fund.

756
00:54:35.269 --> 00:54:35.989
David Mendenhall: Well, I know.

757
00:54:36.309 --> 00:54:38.869
David Mendenhall: I mean, if we can't establish who the rightful owners are.

758
00:54:41.750 --> 00:54:42.230
Kostadis Roussos: No, no, no, no.

759
00:54:42.309 --> 00:54:43.029
Kostadis Roussos: I'm not saying that.

760
00:54:43.670 --> 00:54:52.389
Kostadis Roussos: I'm not saying you should donate it to the city guard retirement fund, where we promise to look aggressively for anyone who might claim this treasure.

761
00:54:52.550 --> 00:54:56.550
Kostadis Roussos: And only if after a suitable amount of time would it fall into the city retirement fund.

762
00:54:56.869 --> 00:55:01.429
Kostadis Roussos: This way, you would know that the money was being guarded and that people would know where to go look for it.

763
00:55:04.620 --> 00:55:06.300
David Mendenhall: Well, I'll think about that.

764
00:55:06.620 --> 00:55:09.019
Kostadis Roussos: I just want you to know that is the if you're concerned.

765
00:55:09.100 --> 00:55:12.860
Kostadis Roussos: I understand your concerns, and that is why I'm very concerned about this.

766
00:55:13.340 --> 00:55:13.900
David Mendenhall: The city guards.

767
00:55:13.980 --> 00:55:17.660
David Mendenhall: You've signed up to be trafficking in stolen goods.

768
00:55:18.539 --> 00:55:20.539
Wade Brown: You haven't signed up for anything yet, have you?

769
00:55:21.740 --> 00:55:22.780
David Mendenhall: Well, I'm desperate.

770
00:55:22.940 --> 00:55:24.300
David Mendenhall: I came to Phandalin for this.

771
00:55:24.539 --> 00:55:27.260
Kostadis Roussos: Oh, no, yeah, this is as you haven't left Neverwinter.

772
00:55:27.420 --> 00:55:28.700
Kostadis Roussos: Now, so now you've leave.

773
00:55:29.260 --> 00:55:29.820
Kostadis Roussos: Yeah, I'm just saying.

774
00:55:31.500 --> 00:55:31.900
Kostadis Roussos: Yes.

775
00:55:32.539 --> 00:55:33.500
Kostadis Roussos: So you arrive.

776
00:55:33.580 --> 00:55:40.300
Kostadis Roussos: So along the way, Brewbarry, you know, is really excited about your musical style, has decided to follow you.

777
00:55:40.460 --> 00:55:43.820
Kostadis Roussos: So, you know, you've acquired him as a traveling companion.

778
00:55:44.140 --> 00:55:45.260
Kostadis Roussos: He's very talkative.

779
00:55:45.580 --> 00:55:45.980
Kostadis Roussos: Not.

780
00:55:47.100 --> 00:55:48.140
David Mendenhall: Is he not talkative?

781
00:55:48.300 --> 00:55:48.860
Gary Young [?]: Is he talkative?

782
00:55:49.740 --> 00:55:50.620
UNKNOWN: With notes.

783
00:55:52.140 --> 00:55:52.860
David Mendenhall: What's that?

784
00:55:53.340 --> 00:55:55.100
Gary Young: Love music with notes.

785
00:55:56.620 --> 00:55:58.380
Gary Young [?]: Does Brewbarry periodically try to sing?

786
00:55:58.700 --> 00:55:59.740
Kostadis Roussos [?]: I agree, Brewbarry.

787
00:55:59.980 --> 00:56:00.539
Kostadis Roussos: Yes.

788
00:56:01.820 --> 00:56:05.500
Kostadis Roussos: And less talking, more music.

789
00:56:05.820 --> 00:56:08.380
Kostadis Roussos: And along the way, you guys encounter.

790
00:56:10.460 --> 00:56:14.380
Kostadis Roussos: So how does this drow show up in all of this?

791
00:56:15.820 --> 00:56:17.660
Kostadis Roussos [?]: I'm not quite sure yet.

792
00:56:20.780 --> 00:56:27.100
Gary Young: So, you know, like she grew up in the Drow cities somewhere.

793
00:56:27.420 --> 00:56:27.820
Gary Young: I don't know.

794
00:56:28.620 --> 00:56:44.860
Kostadis Roussos: Okay, so since you said that, something has happened in the underdark over the last year or so, where the city of the main city of the Drow was completely pulverized by demons that ran wild.

795
00:56:46.060 --> 00:56:47.820
Gary Young: Well, you know, like that tends to happen.

796
00:56:48.300 --> 00:56:49.580
Wade Brown: This wasn't Bob Saget, was it?

797
00:56:49.740 --> 00:56:50.940
Kostadis Roussos: No, no, it was not Bob Saget.

798
00:56:51.180 --> 00:57:03.420
UNKNOWN: I mean, so she could be, you know, that could be what inspired her to become a cleric of the light as she saw the devastation that was brought by worshiping the evil clerics of Lolth and so on and so forth and demonic powers.

799
00:57:03.500 --> 00:57:06.380
UNKNOWN: And, you know, she's had a massive change of heart.

800
00:57:07.340 --> 00:57:08.060
Kostadis Roussos [?]: Change of heart.

801
00:57:08.220 --> 00:57:10.380
Gary Young: Yeah, I'm not sure that's entirely the thing.

802
00:57:10.620 --> 00:57:12.140
Gary Young: Like, I'm not going to go through.

803
00:57:12.300 --> 00:57:18.300
Kostadis Roussos: Or maybe she's unimpressed with their power that they let their chosen people die off in that spectacular way and didn't raise a finger.

804
00:57:20.710 --> 00:57:22.310
Kostadis Roussos [?]: It could just be driven out.

805
00:57:23.270 --> 00:57:24.550
Kostadis Roussos: Okay, driven out.

806
00:57:26.150 --> 00:57:36.230
Gary Young: Yeah, so like maybe she grew up in the dark and then got an exposure to sunlight, having been forced out of the city, and was like, wow, this is great.

807
